            "id": "14084813",
            "headline": "Anthropic IPO: A look at its Indian-origin leaders and expanding India footprint",
            "heading": "Anthropic IPO: A look at its Indian-origin leaders and expanding India footprint",
            "intro": "India is expected to be a key market for Anthropic&#039;s global expansion efforts, as the company looks to tap growing demand for its AI models and enterprise offerings.",
            "body": "Anthropic&#039;s record-breaking IPO debut on November 19 marks a new chapter for the Claude maker, as it becomes the first frontier AI lab to tap the public markets.\r\n\r\nThe AI research lab priced its IPO at $83 per share on November 18, above its earlier marketed range of $70-$78, giving the company a fully diluted valuation of roughly $1.5 trillion.\r\n\r\nThe company and its selling shareholders raised $107.3 billion through an upsized IPO and private placement, including $81.3 billion from the public offering and $26 billion from the private placement.\r\n\r\nAlso Read: Anthropic IPO sparks surge in Indian investor interest, puts GIFT City infrastructure to the test\r\n\r\nSeveral Indian-origin executives have played key roles in Anthropic&#039;s rapid rise, including chief financial officer Krishna Rao, chief technology officer Rahul Patil, and Sid Bidasaria, the co-creator of Claude Code, the company\u2019s agentic coding tool.\r\n\r\nRao, in particular, has played a central role in Anthropic&#039;s fundraising efforts and preparations for its stock market debut, including its IPO roadshow.\r\n\r\nHis nearly 9.5 million shares and stock options have a gross value of about $788 million at the IPO price of $83 per share, according to calculations based on its latest IPO filing. The figure does not account for the cost of exercising the options. Rao is not selling any shares in the offering.\r\n\r\nAlso Read: Krishna Rao: The CFO fueling Anthropic\u2019s race for AI dominance\r\n\r\nWhile Anthropic has not disclosed the individual equity holdings of other Indian-origin executives, they could also see substantial gains from what is poised to be one of the largest IPOs in history.\r\n\r\nAnthropic\u2019s India push\r\n\r\nIndia is also expected to be a key market for Anthropic&#039;s global expansion efforts, as the company looks to tap growing demand for its AI models and enterprise offerings.\r\n\r\nIndia is the second-largest market for Claude.ai, accounting for 7.2 percent of global usage, although that is largely driven by the country&#039;s population size.\r\n\r\nThe company opened its first India office in Bengaluru in February 2026, after announcing its market expansion in October 2025.\r\n\r\nAnthropic India managing director Irina Ghose told Moneycontrol in February that the AI firm&#039;s run-rate revenue had doubled in the country during this period.\r\n\r\nGhose joined the firm in January this year. Since then, Anthropic has substantially expanded its India team to provide applied AI expertise to enterprises, digital natives and startups building on Claude.\r\n\r\nLast month, Anthropic deepened its India push by introducing local inference for its Claude models, including Opus 5, Sonnet 5 and Haiku 4.5, allowing Indian organisations to process AI requests on servers located within the country. .\r\n\r\nThe models are available through Amazon Bedrock, Amazon Web Services\u2019s platform for building generative AI applications and agents at production scale.\r\n\r\nThe rollout, first announced in August, comes amid growing demand for data sovereignty and is aimed at addressing data residency requirements for regulated Indian organisations deploying Claude across sensitive workloads.",
            "posturl": "artificial-intelligence/anthropic-ipo-a-look-at-its-indian-origin-leaders-and-expanding-india-footprint-article-14084813.html",
